  <title>1.8.3: Writing Equations and Isolating Variables</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00190</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.8.3: Writing Equations and Isolating Variables</md:title>
    <md:uuid>68176ea8-0370-44f6-9627-dd897d61e8e2</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="02cd7ec6-2c93-4a63-8c84-579687fa77e9"><problem><para>
      <para>Use the following scenario for questions 1 – 7:</para>
      <para>
        Tank A initially contained 124 liters of water. It is then filled with more water, at a constant rate of 9 liters per minute.
      </para>
      <para>
          How many liters of water are in Tank A after 4 minutes?
        </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here.</para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>160</para><para>
      <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 160</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--Q3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How many liters of water are in Tank A after 4 minutes?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(124+9m\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="64f729a2-90bb-4753-9f2c-0cbf4faf3a14"><problem><para>
      <para>How many minutes, \(m\), have passed when Tank A contains 151 liters of water?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here.</para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>3</para><para>
      <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 3</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--Q7-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How many minutes, \(m\), have passed when Tank A contains \(p\) liters of water?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(\frac{p-124}{9}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>For questions 8 – 14, use the following scenario:</para>
<para>Tank B, which initially contained 80 liters of water, is being drained at a rate of 2.5 liters per minute.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="b69ebe38-b909-4b91-a2be-57d87cd67ce9"><problem><para>
      <para>How many liters of water are in Tank B after 30 seconds?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here.</para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>78.75</para><para>
      <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 78.75</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--Q10-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How many liters of water are in Tank B after \(t\) minutes?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(80 -2.5t\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="faf4b7e6-5040-40d6-acb4-8942ac268566"><problem><para>
      <para>For how many minutes, \(t\), has the water been draining when Tank B contains 75 liters of water?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here.</para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>2</para><para>
      <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 2</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--Q14-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>For how many minutes, \(t\), has the water been draining when Tank B contains v liters of water?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(\frac{80-v}{2.5}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

